course: dictionary
lesson: 14756
title: Proportionate: the right size
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Proportionate is an adjective. It means the right size, amount or degree compared with something else, and fair, not too much. «The fine was proportionate to the damage.» A small scratch gets a small fine and a broken window gets a bigger one. The fine fits the damage.
explain: Look for the words that go with it, such as «proportionate to». Ask: proportionate to what? The sentence tells you what the thing is being compared with. Try swapping in «fair and the right size» and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In maths, proportionate means the amounts grow together in the same way. «Cost is proportionate to weight»: if one thing doubles, the other doubles too. Its opposite is disproportionate, which means too big or too small for the thing it is compared with.
fact: Proportionate means in the right size or amount compared with something else.
fact: «Proportionate to» is a common phrase: «The price is proportionate to the size of the box.»
fact: In maths, two amounts are proportionate when one is always the same multiple of the other.
fact: «A proportionate response» means a reaction that is fair and not too much.
fact: The opposite is disproportionate: «a disproportionate punishment» is far too big, or far too small, for the wrong.
exercise: «The fine was proportionate to the damage: a broken window cost more than a scratch.» What does proportionate mean here? | Paid in coins only | Hidden from everyone | *In the right size compared with the damage | Huge, whatever has happened
exercise: «One pencil costs 20p, so three pencils cost 60p. The cost is proportionate to the number of pencils.» What does this mean? | The cost is a secret | *The cost grows in the same way as the number of pencils | The cost is only for one pencil | The cost stays the same whatever the number
exercise: What is the opposite of proportionate? | Tiny | Fair | *Disproportionate | Proportionate again
test: «A tiny mistake should get a tiny punishment: the punishment should be proportionate to the wrong.» What does this sentence say? | Punishments should be a surprise | Mistakes should be ignored | *The punishment should match the size of the wrong | Punishments should always be as big as possible
test: «A recipe for 2 people needs 100 g of rice, and a recipe for 4 people needs 200 g. The rice is proportionate to the number of people.» How much rice is needed for 6 people? | 600 g | *300 g | 200 g | 100 g
exam: «The judge gave a sentence proportionate to the crime.» Which words best explain proportionate? | Secret and hidden | Sudden and unexpected | *Fair and in the right size | Enormous and cruel
